# The outer wall: Act 1, level 2
# DRAFT by Claude for Matt to mark up (2026-10-13). Built only from pieces that
# exist today. Matt's first answers (2026-10-13): keep the floor that holds
# once, with its combine on the main line; use the roost at 11; two bats and
# an eyeball past the inner gate. The rest is still his to mark up.
ideas: bats on ladders, the sill (a throw from above), the switch a sword holds, the floor that holds once
carried in from the forest: throw and catch, a sword in wood is a ledge

## 1. The culvert's mouth  [breather]  floor 1
Out of the forest's culvert at the foot of the wall. Nothing alive. Five
floors of stone above, with timber in it (hoardings, beams, a postern door),
so the eye learns where a sword will stick before it needs to.
brazier: here

## 2. The first ladder  [introduce: bats on ladders]  floors 1 to 2
One ladder, one bat circling its top half, safe floor under it. Throw from
the foot with the bat across the throw line, or climb while it swings away.
can kill you: the bat
note: a miss costs the walk back to 1, which is a few steps.

## 3. The postern  [introduce: the sill]  floor 2
A skeleton asleep in a low passage, below a step. From the step, a throw
lands in its top. It wakes only when you come close, so a wrong first try
costs nothing.
can kill you: the skeleton, once woken

## 4. The guardroom  [develop: the sill]  floor 2
Two scorpions awake, patrolling a long room with a gallery step at each
end. Fronts armoured. Get to a step before one turns to face you. A chest
in the corner tops the hand back to three, because this fight costs swords.
can kill you: two scorpions
brazier: at the far door

## 5. The tower stair  [develop: bats on ladders]  floors 2 to 4
The first tower. Three ladders stacked, switching sides each storey, a bat
at each landing. Laid out so a missed catch falls to the landing below,
not to the bottom. The stake: a fall from 4 is a climb from 2.
can kill you: three bats

## 6. The wall walk  [introduce: the floor that holds once]  floor 4
The rampart between two towers, timber hoardings over the drop. A side
spur out to a lookout crosses a stretch of planks that creak and hold.
Coming back, they drop you one storey onto the walk below. Nothing waits
there. The player learns the rule by being let off.

## 7. The gatehouse  [introduce: the switch a sword holds]  floor 4
A portcullis across the wall walk. Its switch is a wooden block in a niche,
in plain view across a gap. Throw into it, the gate rises and stays up for
as long as the sword is there. Walk through a sword down. Recall from the
far side and the gate drops behind you.
loop: past the gate, a ladder down the inner face to the brazier at 4

## 7a. The high hoarding  [pocket]  floor 5
Up from the gatehouse roof: scaffolds, two bats, and a fourth sword lying
at the far end. The way back crosses planks that hold once, so the way out
has to be planned as two crossings (a sword left in a beam as the ledge
home is one answer).

## 8. The hoarding chest  [develop: the floor that holds once]  floor 3
The chest the next fight needs sits at the end of a hoarding over spikes.
The planks hold going out and drop coming back. Put a sword in a beam on
the way out to stand on coming home, or jump it if you can.
can kill you: the spikes

## 9. The bailey  [breather]  floors 1 to 2
Kept from today. The caged dragon, seen whole behind bars, in a quiet
stretch with no puzzle in it.
brazier: at the bailey gate

## 10. The bailey yard  [develop: the switch a sword holds]  floor 2
Kept from today: a switch raises the gate ahead. Now there are two bats
round the ladder past the gate. Hold the gate with one sword and you climb
into a fight with two against two.
can kill you: two bats

## 11. The barbican stair  [twist: bats on ladders]  floors 1 to 3
A ladder with nothing on it. The weapon racks along it are a roost: the
bats wake when you are halfway up, below you and above you. The answer that
worked (throw from the foot) is behind you now. Drop off, fight from the
floor, climb again.
can kill you: the roost

## 12. The bailey wall  [twist: the floor that holds once]  floors 2 to 1
The only way down to the ditch is a run of planks that hold once. Until
now they were a trap; here you want them to drop. Cross, turn, come back,
and fall through to the ditch's edge.

## 13. The ditch  [combine: bats on ladders + a sword is a ledge]  floor 1
Kept from today. Crossed by standing on your own sword, under a bat.
can kill you: the bat, the ditch

## 14. The barbican gallery  [twist: the sill]  floor 3
A step above a sleeping scorpion, as at 3, but an eyeball hangs where the
sword comes home. The throw from the step kills; the catch does not, unless
you move while it is in the air so its line home misses the eye. A fight
decided on the move.
can kill you: the scorpion, the eyeball
loop: a door from the gallery back down to the brazier at 9

## 15. The inner gate  [twist: the switch a sword holds]  floor 3
Two switches hold one gate, the second thrown at while standing on the
sword in the first. Past it, the room needs every sword you have. The
answer that worked (leave a sword holding it) is wrong here: go through,
recall both, and the gate shuts behind you. The way back is the barbican
door at 14.
can kill you: two bats and an eyeball
brazier: past the gate

## 16. The plinth approach  [combine: the floor that holds once + the sill]  floors 2 to 1
Planks that hold once over the gate yard, with a scorpion patrolling below.
Drop through them in behind it, on its unarmoured side, and throw before it
turns. Or stay up and throw down from the planks' end, as at the sill.
can kill you: the scorpion, if you land facing it

## 17. The gate yard  [combine: the sill + the switch a sword holds]  floor 1
Kept from today: the castle on a plinth whose face is the switch. A scorpion
patrols the plinth top in front of the gate. Kill it from the yard wall's
step, then hold the gate with a sword in the plinth's face and walk out.
The exit is one way.
can kill you: the scorpion

# As built (N3, 2026-10-14)
# Where levels/act1_wall.level differs from the sheet above, and why. Each is
# Claude's call, made unattended, for Matt to overrule.
# - 7: the loop back to the brazier at 4 goes through a portcullis whose
#   switch is on the corridor side, so it opens for a player coming round
#   and cannot be used to skip the tower on the way out.
# - 8: a quick double jump (land on the shaking planks, jump to the post)
#   gets home without the sword, as "or jump it if you can" allows. Widen the
#   pit if the sword should be the only answer.
# - 13: a brazier past the ditch, as the old wall had.
# - 14: a throw that kills is lost, so there is no catch to spoil. The eye
#   drifts to your height and sits on the line from the step: throw before it
#   settles and the skeleton dies; wait and the eye takes the sword. The loop
#   is the ladder down to the ditch's brazier (13), not the bailey's (9).
# - 15: past the gate, a ladder up through the roof drops you back to the
#   ditch's brazier, since the gate shuts once you recall your swords.
# - 16: the "throw down from the planks' end" answer is not built (throws are
#   flat); the other way in is a ladder down that lands you facing the
#   scorpion. The planks end on stone so coming back is a second landing.
#   The planks start a cell short of that ladder (2026-10-24): a one-cell
#   hole beside them was narrower than the hero, so the climb back up
#   stopped under the planks (and no one fitted down it either). The hole
#   is two cells now: a climb back up, a drop that lands you facing the
#   scorpion as the note above meant, and a hop onto the planks going out.
#   Walk-back knows the hero's width now.
# - 17: "the yard wall's step" is a step on a pillar in the yard, level with
#   the plinth's top plus a sill.
